question:
Array A contains 256 elements of 4 bytes each. Its first element is stored at physical address 4,096.
Array B contains 512 elements of 4 bytes each. Its first element is stored at physical address 8,192.
Assume that only arrays A and B can be cached in an initially empty, physically addressed, physically tagged, direct-mapped, 2K-byte cache with an 8-byte block size. The following loop is then executed.
for (i = 0; i < 256; i++)
        A[i] = A[i] + B[2*i];
During the execution of the loop, how many bytes will be written to memory if the cache has a write-back policy?    A. 0    B. 256    C. 1024    D. 2000

Array A contains 256 elements of 4 bytes each. Its first element is stored at physical address 4,096.
Array B contains 512 elements of 4 bytes each. Its first element is stored at physical address 8,192.
Assume that only arrays A and B can be cached in an initially empty, physically addressed, physically tagged, direct-mapped, 2K-byte cache with an 8-byte block size. The following loop is then executed.
for (i = 0; i < 256; i++)
        A[i] = A[i] + B[2*i];
During the execution of the loop, how many bytes will be written to memory if the cache has a write-through policy?    A. 0    B. 256    C. 1024    D. 2048

Let R be a ring and let U and V be (two-sided) ideals of R. Which of the following must also be ideals of R ?
I. {u + v : u \in and v \in V}
II. {uv : u \in U and v \in V}
III. {x : x \in U and x \in V}?    A. II only    B. III only    C. I and II only    D. I and III only
